Section 6 — Bloch Sphere Breakdown
This is one of the most visually striking sections. In your superposition notebook, the Bloch sphere was the go-to visualization: every single-qubit pure state maps to a unique point on the surface of the sphere.
For the product state, you see qubit 0 at the +X equator (that's ∣+⟩|+\rangle
∣+⟩) and qubit 1 at the north pole (that's ∣0⟩|0\rangle
∣0⟩). Clean, informative, exactly what you'd expect.

For the Bell state, both vectors collapse to the dead center of the sphere — the origin. This looks like the qubits are in no state at all, but that's not what's happening. Each qubit's reduced state is the maximally mixed state 12I\frac{1}{2}I
21​I, which has no preferred direction. The Bloch sphere can only represent single-qubit states, and for an entangled qubit, the single-qubit description is genuinely incomplete. The information about the system exists entirely in the correlations between the two qubits, which lives in a 4-dimensional Hilbert space that the 3D Bloch sphere can't capture.

This is a good conceptual anchor: the Bloch sphere's failure mode tells you exactly where the entanglement "lives" — not in either qubit, but in the space between them.
Section 7 — Shot-by-Shot Correlations
This section makes entanglement visceral by printing individual measurement outcomes from 20 shots with memory=True. Instead of aggregate statistics, you see each shot: qubit 0 got 0 and qubit 1 got 0, qubit 0 got 1 and qubit 1 got 1, etc. Every single row matches. The match rate is 20/20 (or whatever the shot count is).
The code handles Qiskit's bit ordering convention, which stores the most significant bit first in the outcome string. So for a 2-qubit outcome string like '01', the left digit is qubit 1 and the right digit is qubit 0. The code parses this correctly with outcome[0] for qubit 1 and outcome[1] for qubit 0.
The explanatory note after the code addresses the obvious question: if measuring one qubit instantly determines the other, can you use this for faster-than-light communication? No, because each individual measurement is still uniformly random. Alice can't choose her outcome, so she can't encode a message. The correlation only becomes apparent when Alice and Bob compare their results afterward, which requires classical communication.
Section 8 — Measuring in Different Bases
This is where the notebook transitions from "entanglement is weird" to "entanglement is provably non-classical."
Classical correlations (like two coins that were secretly set to match before being sealed in envelopes) can explain same-basis correlations perfectly. If someone secretly put matching coins in two envelopes, you'd always see them agree when you open both. That's not surprising.
The quantum twist: the Bell state ∣Φ+⟩|\Phi^+\rangle
∣Φ+⟩ stays correlated even when you change the measurement basis. The X-basis measurement is implemented by applying a Hadamard to both qubits before measuring (since H rotates the Z-basis into the X-basis). The histogram shows that in the X-basis, you *also* get only ∣00⟩|00\rangle
∣00⟩ and ∣11⟩|11\rangle
∣11⟩ (meaning both qubits agree in the X-basis too).

No pre-set classical strategy can produce perfect correlations in both the Z and X bases simultaneously. If the coins were pre-set to agree in the Z-basis, they'd show random results in the X-basis. This is the intuitive version of Bell's theorem, and it's what the next section formalizes.
