Section 10 — Superdense Coding
This section pivots from "why is entanglement weird" to "why is entanglement useful." Superdense coding is a protocol where Alice and Bob pre-share a Bell pair, and then Alice can send 2 classical bits of information by transmitting only 1 qubit.
The protocol works because there are exactly four Bell states, and Alice can transform the shared ∣Φ+⟩|\Phi^+\rangle
∣Φ+⟩ into any of the four by applying operations to *her qubit only*:


00: do nothing (identity). State stays ∣Φ+⟩|\Phi^+\rangle
∣Φ+⟩.

01: apply X. State becomes ∣Ψ+⟩|\Psi^+\rangle
∣Ψ+⟩.

10: apply Z. State becomes ∣Φ−⟩|\Phi^-\rangle
∣Φ−⟩.

11: apply ZX. State becomes ∣Ψ−⟩|\Psi^-\rangle
∣Ψ−⟩.


Alice then sends her qubit to Bob. Bob now has both qubits and can perform a Bell measurement (reverse the Bell circuit: CNOT then H) to determine which of the four Bell states he has, which tells him Alice's 2-bit message. The notebook verifies all four messages decode correctly.
This works because the four Bell states are orthogonal — they're perfectly distinguishable with the right measurement. Alice's single-qubit operations rotate through the full Bell basis, encoding 2 bits of information into the choice of Bell state.
